  "summary": "Seven preprints from 12–19 November 2026 forecast how 21-cm, CMB, time-delay and pulsar timing data can discriminate LCDM from alternative models.",
  "synthesis": "Most items quantify how well existing or forthcoming datasets can discriminate LCDM from alternatives, using forecasts and new statistics rather than new measurements. In 21-cm cosmology, persistent homology of Cosmic Dawn brightness maps, the phase gradient spectrum for the squeezed bispectrum, and SKA1-Low forecasts for boosted dark matter each target a distinct observable. Bayesian forecasts for Simons Observatory and CMB-S4 ask whether w0waCDM can be separated from LCDM shadow models. The time-delay forecast finds that mass-sheet transformation uncertainties reduce the significance of the decreasing H0 trend to ~1σ. The binaryfog surrogate tests how astrophysical priors affect evidence for new-physics sources in pulsar timing arrays. The Shapley jet-alignment result (4.5σ) is a separate observational large-scale-structure claim.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-22"
}
